1. The longest common subsequence (LCS) of two strings is the longest subsequence shared by both strings. A subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from another sequence by deleting some or no elements without changing the order of the remaining elements. For example, the longest common subsequence of "ABCDGH" and "AEDFHR" is "ADH" of length 3.
2. You can use dynamic programming to solve this problem efficiently. The key idea is to construct a table of size m × n, where m and n are the lengths of the two input strings. The table contains the longest common subsequence of the first i characters of the first string and the first j characters of the second string. The base cases are when either i or j is 0, in which case the longest common subsequence is empty.
3. To fill in the rest of the table, you can use a recursive approach where each cell is computed based on the values in the cells above and to the left. For example, to compute the value in cell (i, j), you can check if the last character of the first string matches the last character of the second string. If they match, then you can add 1 to the value in the cell above and to the left (i-1, j-1). Otherwise, you can take the maximum of the values in the cells above and to the left (i-1, j) and (i, j-1).
4. Once you have filled in the entire table, you can find the longest common subsequence by following a path from the bottom right corner to the top left corner, taking the maximum value at each step. The length of this path will be the length of the longest common subsequence.

