1. A binary search algorithm is an efficient algorithm for finding an element from a sorted array.
2. The algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half.
3. The algorithm starts by comparing the middle element of the array with the target element.
4. If the target element is greater than the middle element, the search continues in the upper half of the array.
5. If the target element is less than the middle element, the search continues in the lower half of the array.
6. The algorithm continues until the target element is found or the search interval is empty.
7. The time complexity of the binary search algorithm is O(log n), where n is the size of the array.

The following code implements a binary search algorithm in JavaScript:

function binarySearch(array, target) {
let left = 0;
let right = array.length - 1;

while (left <= right) {
let mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
if (array[mid] === target) {
return mid;
} else if (array[mid] < target) {
left = mid + 1;
} else {
right = mid - 1;
}
}

return -1;
}

In this implementation, the left and right pointers are initialized to the beginning and end of the array, respectively. The while loop continues as long as the left pointer is less than or equal to the right pointer.

The middle element is calculated using the Math.floor() function, which rounds the result down to the nearest integer. The if statement checks if the target element is found at the middle index. If not, the if-else statement updates the left or right pointer accordingly.

The binary search algorithm has a time complexity of O(log n), where n is the size of the array. This means that the algorithm's running time grows logarithmically with the size of the array, making it an efficient algorithm for searching a sorted array.

